WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:50.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. On a recent trip to Puerto Rico, producer Jacob Medina Gleason was introduced to a piece of family history. It's an old manila folder with a photo of her grandfather, Tomas Vélez Lópezpez attached to the front. In the picture, Tomás is a young 20-something with thick wavy hair and a full Tom Selleck mustache. And across the top of the folder, in big bold letters, it reads, The Division of Special Investigations and it has his case number. I don't know if they started from one, but if they did, he was 7,480. The intelligence division opened a case on Jaka's grandpa in the 1970s.
这里是《99% Invisible》。我是Roman Mars。在最近的一次波多黎各之行中，制片人Jacob Medina Gleason接触到了一段家族史。那是一个旧的马尼拉纸文件夹，封面贴着她祖父Tomas Vélez López的照片。照片里的Tomas是一位20多岁的年轻人，留着浓密的波浪发和标志性的汤姆·塞立克式胡子。文件夹顶部用醒目的大字写着“特别调查处”，并标注了他的案件编号。我不知道他们是不是从1号开始编号的，但如果是的话，他是第7480号。情报部门在20世纪70年代为Jaca的祖父立了案。

2
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:13.000
The folder contains over 60 pages of notes and details about Tomas's life. Mundane things that are common in police files. His height, his weight, his license plate number. But there were other, less mundane details too. There's a line here that says, peligroso no. license plate number, but there were other less mundane details too. There's a line here that says, peligroso? No. So it's, is he dangerous?
文件夹里包含了60多页关于Tomas生平的笔记和细节。这些都是警察档案中常见的琐事。他的身高、体重、车牌号。但也有其他不那么寻常的细节。这里有一行字写着“危险吗？不”。所以，这是在问他是否危险。

3
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:57.000
And no, he apparently was not dangerous. Flipping through the folder, it was clear that my grandpa was part of a big investigation, but as I kept reading through the documents, I couldn't find what crime he was being accused of, or any allegations at all. Instead what I learned is that his case was about something else entirely. The file sums it up in one line. You know they're saying sort of purpose of the investigation and it's to determine his activities politically. This file the police kept on my grandpa is a type of folder known in Puerto Rico as a Carpeta. Carpetas were created to spy on supporters of Puerto Rican independence, and the folders
显然，他并不危险。翻阅文件夹时，很明显我祖父卷入了一场大规模调查，但随着我继续阅读这些文件，我找不到他被指控的罪名，甚至没有任何指控。相反，我了解到他的案件完全是另一回事。档案用一行字总结了调查目的，即确定他的政治活动。警察为我祖父保留的这份档案在波多黎各被称为“Carpeta”（档案夹）。这些档案夹是为了监视波多黎各独立运动的支持者而建立的，而这些文件夹

4
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:37.000
were one part of a decades-long surveillance operation which tracked more than 150,000 Puerto Ricans. The goal of the government's surveillance project was to intimidate and suppress the political movement, one that Jaca's grandfather belonged to. Your grandpa was a dreamer. And his first dream was having his nation free, was having his nation free, free from the power of the United States. That was his first love. When I met him, he loved it more than me. Truly. I mean, truly.
是长达数十年的监视行动的一部分，追踪了超过15万名波多黎各人。政府监视项目的目标是恐吓和镇压Jaca祖父所属的政治运动。“你祖父是个梦想家。他最初的梦想是让国家获得自由，让国家摆脱美国的力量。那是他的初恋。当我遇见他时，他爱那个梦想胜过爱我。真的，我是说真的。”

5
00:02:37.000 --> 00:03:13.000
This is my grandma Nancy. If you can't already tell, Abuela Nancy is New Yorkican, down to her bones. And she told me that she actually met my grandpa because of the independence movement. We both sold periodico claridad. The two of them first ran into each other selling a pro-independence newspaper on the same street corner. Then one day he told me, hey, you can't take my corner. You gotta go sell somewhere else. That was like, I said, well, uh-uh.
这是我的祖母Nancy。如果你还没看出来，Abuela Nancy骨子里就是个地道的纽约波多黎各人。她告诉我，她其实是因为独立运动才认识我祖父的。“我们都卖《Claridad》报。”他们俩第一次相遇是在同一条街角兜售支持独立的报纸。“有一天他告诉我，嘿，你不能占我的地盘。你得去别处卖。我说，嗯，才不呢。”

6
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:46.000
That's how it all started. We had a very, very sweet friendship. My grandma also told me that after their little meet-cute, the government opened a carpeta on her, too. They started surveilling me when we two got together. Mine wasn't even as big as his. My grandpa Tomás died years ago, so I never got to ask him about any of this. But what I do know is that he, like a lot of Puerto Ricans, served in the US Army.
一切就是这样开始的。我们有着非常非常甜蜜的友谊。祖母还告诉我，在他们那次可爱的邂逅之后，政府也为她建立了一个档案夹。“我们在一起后，他们就开始监视我了。我的档案还没他的大呢。”我祖父Tomas多年前就去世了，所以我从未有机会问他这些事。但我知道的是，像许多波多黎各人一样，他曾在美军服役。

7
00:03:46.000 --> 00:04:09.000
It was not lost on him that despite being a US citizen, Puerto Rico was not really a part of the United States. So he spent his life right up until his later years fighting for Puerto Rican independence. We were activists. We were always activists. Wherever there was something to do and something to fight for, we were there.
他很清楚，尽管身为美国公民，波多黎各并不是美国真正的一部分。所以他一生直到晚年都在为波多黎各的独立而战。“我们是活动家。我们一直是活动家。无论哪里有事可做，有仗可打，我们就在那里。”

8
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:42.000
For years, decades even, my grandpa's carpeta wasn't sitting in some government building or archive, but was buried in the back of my abuela's closet. All over Puerto Rico, thousands of these carpetas are lying around, perhaps in some other abuelas' closet. And each one, in its own way, provides a glimpse into the long and often ugly relationship between Puerto Rico and the United States. Puerto Rico has been a colony since the Spanish first arrived on the island of Burinque 500
多年来，甚至几十年来，我祖父的档案夹并没有放在什么政府大楼或档案馆里，而是埋在我祖母衣柜的深处。在整个波多黎各，成千上万这样的档案夹散落在各处，也许就在其他祖母的衣柜里。每一个档案夹都以其独特的方式，让我们得以一窥波多黎各与美国之间漫长且往往丑陋的关系。自500年前西班牙人首次抵达布林克岛（Burinque）以来，波多黎各一直是一个殖民地。

9
00:04:42.000 --> 00:05:20.000
years ago. And from the very beginning, resistance defined the identity of the island of Burinque 500 years ago, and from the very beginning, resistance defined the identity of the island. In fact, the very idea of being Puerto Rican was born out of this tension between the colonized and the colonizer. The first time that the term Puerto Rican appears in the Spanish colonial record, it is in the early 1700s when a military official is complaining about all these Puerto Ricans, bastards of Spanish military generals. And so it is used to refer to Puerto Ricans, people from the archipelago, as bastards.
从一开始，抵抗就定义了这个岛屿的身份。事实上，“波多黎各人”这一概念正是诞生于被殖民者与殖民者之间的这种张力之中。在西班牙殖民记录中，“波多黎各人”一词最早出现在18世纪初，当时一位军事官员抱怨这些“波多黎各人”，称他们是西班牙军事将领的私生子。因此，它被用来指代波多黎各人，即来自群岛的人，意为“私生子”。

10
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:50.000
This is Jorrell Meléndez Padillo. He's an assistant professor of Latin American and Caribbean history at the University of Wisconsin Madison and author of the book Puerto Rico, A National History. Jorrel says that despite the Spanish crown's original intent to demean, people on the island embraced the term Puerto Rican, using it to rally around a shared identity. I think that Puerto Rican identity has always been constructed in opposition to colonialism.
这是Jorrell Meléndez Padillo。他是威斯康星大学麦迪逊分校拉丁美洲和加勒比历史系的助理教授，也是《波多黎各：一部国家史》一书的作者。Jorrel说，尽管西班牙王室最初的意图是贬低，但岛上的人们接受了这个词，并用它来凝聚共同的身份认同。“我认为波多黎各人的身份认同一直是在反殖民主义的对立面中构建出来的。”

11
00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:16.000
It is an affirmation of being something different from Spain. In the 19th century, hostility between the Spanish Empire and its colonies sparked a number of revolutions throughout the Caribbean. Puerto Ricans saw uprisings taking place all around them in the Dominican Republic and Cuba, and wanted in on the action. Which is when the United States entered the equation. In 1898, during the Spanish-American War,
这是对“与西班牙不同”的一种肯定。19世纪，西班牙帝国与其殖民地之间的敌意在整个加勒比地区引发了多次革命。波多黎各人看到多米尼加共和国和古巴周围发生的起义，也想参与其中。这时，美国介入了。1898年，在美西战争期间，

12
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:36.000
the United States presented themselves as liberators to the Puerto Rican people. But by the time the dust settled and the US had beaten the Spanish, they did not give Puerto Rico independence. Instead, they annexed it. At the time when a US territory was annexed, the next step for it was statehood. That's just how territories had worked since the founding of the country.
美国以解放者的姿态出现在波多黎各人民面前。但当尘埃落定，美国击败西班牙后，他们并没有给予波多黎各独立，而是将其吞并了。当时，当一个美国领土被吞并时，下一步通常就是成为州。这就是该国建国以来领土运作的方式。

13
00:06:36.000 --> 00:07:11.000
A swath of land would be taken, a local government would form, and the territory would become a state. But, when the US acquired the Philippines,, Guam and Puerto Rico, lots of Americans were like, I don't know about all that. Americans in general did not like the idea of giving statehood to places inhabited by a majority of people who they didn't perceive as white. This is Christina Bonsacraos, a professor of constitutional law at Columbia Law School. They didn't think that the people of these islands could be properly assimilated into American culture,
一块土地被占领，建立地方政府，然后领土成为一个州。但是，当美国获得菲律宾、关岛和波多黎各时，许多美国人表示怀疑。美国人普遍不喜欢将州权授予那些他们认为非白人占多数的地方。这是哥伦比亚大学法学院宪法学教授Christina Bonsacraos。“他们不认为这些岛屿上的人民能够适当地融入美国文化，”

14
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:39.000
and yet instead of just leaving them alone, the United States took them anyway. And so then we've got this debate. What are we going to do? Should we have them as territories? Why were we annexing them? What's going to happen next? These questions went all the way up to the Supreme Court in a series of cases called the Insular Cases. In these arguments, the court debated whether or not the Constitution applied to all of these newly annexed territories. And the answer to that was a resounding sort of?
然而，美国并没有放任他们，还是占领了他们。于是就有了这场辩论：我们该怎么办？应该让他们作为领土吗？我们为什么要吞并他们？接下来会发生什么？这些问题一直闹到了最高法院，引发了一系列被称为“岛屿案件”的诉讼。在这些辩论中，法院讨论了宪法是否适用于所有这些新吞并的领土。而答案是一种响亮的“算是吧？”

15
00:07:39.000 --> 00:08:10.000
It ended up essentially saying that Puerto Rico was not part of the United States in a more general sense. But it also said that Puerto Rico wasn't foreign. And so it came up with this language. Puerto Rico and the other territories by implication, that is the other new ones, the Philippines and Guam, are foreign to the United States in a domestic sense. And also, the court said, they are domestic in a domestic sense. And also the court said they are domestic in an international sense.
最终，法院实质上认为波多黎各在更广泛的意义上不是美国的一部分。但它也说波多黎各不是外国。于是它提出了这种措辞：波多黎各和其他领土（暗示指菲律宾和关岛）在“国内意义上”是外国，同时法院又说，它们在“国际意义上”是国内的。

16
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:41.000
If foreign in a domestic sense and domestic in an international sense is complete gibberish to you, that's because it is. Basically, the case is determined that the Constitution only applied to Puerto Rico as the U.S. saw fit because it wasn't a state, and then used this obscure language to explain away why it wouldn't become one. It's a two-step process. Annex and incorporate. And they were not planning to incorporate Puerto Rico. The Foraker Act and the Jones Act gave Puerto Ricans the ability to elect their own legislature and granted citizenship.
如果“国内意义上的外国”和“国际意义上的国内”对你来说完全是胡言乱语，那是因为它确实如此。基本上，这些案件判定宪法仅在美方认为合适时才适用于波多黎各，因为它不是一个州，然后用这种晦涩的语言来解释为什么它不会成为一个州。这是一个两步走的过程：吞并和合并。而他们并不打算合并波多黎各。《福勒法案》和《琼斯法案》赋予了波多黎各人选举自己立法机构的权利，并授予了公民身份。

17
00:08:41.000 --> 00:09:18.000
So Puerto Ricans were citizens, but not totally citizens. And they could hold elections, but they still had no representation in Congress. As you could imagine, all this vague, incoherent language pissed a lot of people off. Puerto Rico had been a colony for hundreds of years by this time, and their fate was always being decided for them by outside forces. And within Puerto Rico, no one voiced their frustration with the island's status more loudly than the leader of the Nationalist Party, Pedro Alviso Campos. It was very similar to the Puerto Ricans. The Puerto Ricans should not be born.
所以波多黎各人是公民，但不是完全的公民。他们可以举行选举，但在国会中仍然没有代表权。你可以想象，所有这些模糊、不连贯的措辞激怒了很多人。到那时，波多黎各已经做了几百年的殖民地，他们的命运总是由外部力量决定。在波多黎各内部，没有人比民族主义党领袖Pedro Albizu Campos更强烈地表达对岛屿地位的不满了。“这和波多黎各人非常相似。波多黎各人不应该出生。”

18
00:09:18.000 --> 00:10:14.000
Yes, but when the black people are the sons of the Yankees, we need the Puerto Ricans to put their backpacks on top of them so they can go and kill themselves. Starting in the 1930s, Albizu Campos led the Nationalist Party, which was one of the strongest voices in the Puerto Rican independence movement. Albizu Campos is a fascinating individual. Pedro Albizu Campos was a Harvard-educated lawyer, Afro-descendant. He had great rhetoric. He would spit fire. He dominated a crowd when he spoke and he spoke about the ills of Puerto Rico. A lot of what Albiso Campos was responding to came from Supreme Court cases that set up the political dynamic between the U.S. and Puerto Rico.
“是的，但当黑人是洋基佬的儿子时，我们需要波多黎各人把背包背在他们身上，这样他们就可以去自杀。”从20世纪30年代开始，Albizu Campos领导了民族主义党，这是波多黎各独立运动中最强有力的声音之一。“Albizu Campos是一个迷人的人物。Pedro Albizu Campos是一位受过哈佛教育的律师，是非洲裔。他有很强的修辞能力。他会喷火。他演讲时能掌控全场，他谈论波多黎各的弊病。”Albizu Campos回应的很多内容都来自确立美波政治动态的最高法院案件。

19
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:42.000
He wanted Puerto Ricans to see that this relationship made them second class citizens, and he wanted them to get mad. There were peaceful protests that shined a light on Puerto Rico's colonial status. But in 1935, Puerto Rican police, under the control of the U.S., killed a number of independence protesters. Months later, in response, some in the independence movement killed the police chief. After that, the U.S. determined that the Nationalist Party wasn't a political party, but a bunch
他希望波多黎各人看到这种关系使他们成为二等公民，并希望他们感到愤怒。当时有和平抗议活动揭露了波多黎各的殖民地位。但在1935年，受美国控制的波多黎各警察杀害了一些独立抗议者。几个月后，作为回应，独立运动中的一些人杀死了警察局长。此后，美国认定民族主义党不是一个政党，而是一群

20
00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:09.000
of insurrectionists. And they were not afraid to use violence. And that is something that authorities feared. At first, Alviso Campos and the Nationalist Party tried to seek change through the ballot box, but the scales were always tipped against them. The US held a firm grip over the electoral system on the island, and basically every other lever of power too. And so, Alvizu Campos determined that they couldn't gain independence within a system
暴乱分子。他们不惧怕使用暴力。而这正是当局所担心的。起初，Albizu Campos和民族主义党试图通过投票箱寻求变革，但天平总是向他们倾斜。美国牢牢控制着岛上的选举制度，基本上也控制着其他所有的权力杠杆。因此，Albizu Campos断定他们无法在一个旨在压迫他们的系统中获得独立。

21
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:35.000
designed to oppress them. He declared that the Nationalist Party would withdraw from electoral politics so long as the U.S. was in power. Freedom wasn't going to be earned. They needed to take it. In the 1930s, hoping to restore order to the island, the United States appointed a new governor to oversee Puerto Rico. He militarized the police and jailed political opponents, including Albi-Sucampos.
他宣布，只要美国还在掌权，民族主义党就会退出选举政治。自由不是靠乞求得来的，他们必须夺取它。20世纪30年代，为了恢复岛上的秩序，美国任命了一位新总督来监管波多黎各。他将警察军事化，并监禁了包括Albizu Campos在内的政治对手。

22
00:11:35.000 --> 00:12:06.000
The governor also authorized a new surveillance operation, one that would keep tabs on people they suspected to be part of the independence movement. This was the beginning of the Carpetas. Basically the way it would work was, it was sort of like a three strike system. That's Christopher Gregory Rivera. He's a photographer and artist who's done extensive research on Carpetas. He says that the government had a whole process for determining who was deemed suspicious enough to have one opened on them.
总督还授权了一项新的监视行动，旨在监视那些被怀疑参与独立运动的人。这就是“档案夹”（Carpetas）的开端。“基本上它的运作方式就像一个三振出局系统。”这是Christopher Gregory Rivera。他是一位摄影师和艺术家，对档案夹进行了广泛的研究。他说，政府有一整套流程来确定谁被认为足够可疑，从而需要为他们建立档案。

23
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:35.000
If I was a heavily surveilled person and we had a coffee, they would open an index card on you. And then if you never got any more cards, then you'd probably kind of fall off the radar. But if they saw you with somebody else or they saw you at a rally, you'd get another card. And sort of through that process, they kept track of connections. And when you got three of these index cards, then they would actually formally open a carpeta on you.
“如果我是一个被严密监视的人，我们一起喝了杯咖啡，他们就会为你建立一张索引卡。如果你之后再也没有收到更多的卡片，你可能就会从雷达上消失。但如果他们看到你和别人在一起，或者看到你参加集会，你就会得到另一张卡片。通过这个过程，他们追踪了人际关系。当你集齐三张索引卡时，他们就会正式为你建立一个档案夹。”

24
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:53.000
At the time, it was generally known within the independence movement that the government was watching. But how close and in what ways remained a mystery? The police, of course, didn't have the manpower to surveil each and every person who sympathized with the cause. And so they paid friends, neighbors, and colleagues to do a lot of the dirty work.
当时，独立运动内部普遍知道政府在监视。但监视的程度和方式仍然是个谜。当然，警察没有人力去监视每一个同情该事业的人。所以他们花钱雇佣朋友、邻居和同事来做很多肮脏的工作。

25
00:12:53.000 --> 00:13:21.000
This, of course, created an obvious incentive. If you had something to say, you get paid. Or if you were in trouble with the law for whatever reason, you could get out of trouble by helping the police. And at a time when work was hard to come by on the island, lots of people suddenly had lots to say. From my perspective, having read a lot of them and talking to people who were surveilled and scholars and other researchers, you know, a lot of the information is like made up.
这当然产生了一种明显的激励机制。如果你有话要说，你就能得到报酬。或者如果你因为某种原因惹上了法律麻烦，你可以通过帮助警察来摆脱困境。在岛上工作难找的时候，很多人突然间有了很多话要说。“从我的角度来看，读过很多档案，并与被监视者、学者和其他研究人员交谈后，你知道，很多信息都是编造的。”

26
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:50.000
Christopher says that despite the bad information, the Carpetas still served a purpose for the Puerto Rican government. It had the effect of making people in the independence movement feel watched, which made organizing harder. The Carpetas were an off-the-book means of suppression while the state created on-the-book ways to criminalize their activity. And the size of the Carpeta indicated how aggressively a person was being surveilled. A person with a small Carpeta might never see a cop, while a person with a three-foot-tall carpeta might
